<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Hejaz</h1><p class="st-meta">1916 CE – 1925 CE · capital Mecca</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Hashemite kingdom proclaimed by Sharif Hussein during the Arab Revolt, which held Mecca and Medina until conquered by Ibn Saud.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In June 1916 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-bin-ali-king-of-hejaz">Hussein ibn Ali</a>, head of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sharifate-of-mecca">Sharifate of Mecca</a>, launched the Arab Revolt against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> with British support, encouraged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mcmahon-hussein-correspondence">Hussein-McMahon correspondence</a>. He proclaimed himself King of the Arab Lands; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> recognised him as King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>. His sons Faisal and Abdullah led Arab forces north.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>, its holy cities, the pilgrimage and the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeddah">Jeddah</a>, financed largely by British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> and pilgrim fees. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-bin-ali-king-of-hejaz">Hussein</a> refused to accept the post-war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">mandates</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, quarrelled with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> and declined to sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>. In March 1924, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>, he declared himself caliph.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud's</a> Ikhwan took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif">Ta'if</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in late 1924. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-bin-ali-king-of-hejaz">Hussein</a> abdicated in favour of his son Ali, who surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeddah">Jeddah</a> in December 1925. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hejaz-and-nejd">Kingdom of Hejaz and Nejd</a>, while Hussein's sons ruled the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-iraq">Kingdom of Iraq</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-transjordan">Emirate of Transjordan</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-hejaz.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Hejaz" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Orange Tuesday at English Wikipedia / (Original text: Orange Tuesday (talk)) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hejaz_(1917).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1916 CE – 1925 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mecca</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hussein ibn Ali, Ali ibn Hussein</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Saudi Arabia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hashemite Kingdom of Hejaz, Hejaz</dd></dl></aside></div>
